The fact-check found that during the 2020 presidential debate, Donald Trump opposed a federal $15 minimum wage, stating it would be "ruinous" and should be a state decision, while expressing openness to $15 for "some states" ([NBC News](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2020-election/wages-was-most-searched-issue-google-during-final-trump-biden-n1244547), [Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/view/articles/2020-10-23/a-15-minimum-wage-would-hurt-biden-s-economy)). However, no evidence was found of Trump using the exact phrase "too much" or specifically linking a $15 wage to essential workers; his separate opposition to hazard pay for essential workers was noted but not tied to the $15 claim ([CWA Union](https://cwa-union.org/trumps-anti-worker-record)). Therefore, the claim cannot be conclusively supported or refuted, leading to a verdict of `not enough information`.